   - Intended internal modules under VanillaExpanded/src/CraftingMenu: client catalogue/output identity, search/order and selection state, ingredient allocator with ordinary/shift objectives, transfer planner, native client inventory-operation coordinator, panel integration, and popout renderer. This is a responsibility map, not a requirement for one file per responsibility.
   - Intended test locations: VanillaExpanded.Tests/Unit/CraftingMenu and, for actual integration cases, VanillaExpanded.Tests/E2E/CraftingMenu; the E2E folder does not yet exist. Use Category=Unit/Category=E2E and existing mocks/real InventoryGeneric patterns. Existing [AlloyDepositSystemTests.cs](../VanillaExpanded.Tests/Unit/AlloyCalculator/AlloyDepositSystemTests.cs) provides network-fixture conventions.
   - No custom catalogue/placement packets or server-side handlers are selected. [VanillaExpandedModSystem.cs](../VanillaExpanded/src/VanillaExpandedModSystem.cs) remains registration/Harmony lifecycle prior art; remove client feature subscriptions/resources on Dispose/LeaveWorld.
   - Catalogue initialization uses IClientEventAPI.LevelFinalize once player/client recipes are ready; LeaveWorld provides teardown. The user subsequently confirmed world-loading-only rebuilds (2026-10-29), superseding the earlier registry-change-observer requirement. Recipes are immutable while loaded; do not add a registry observer, server manifest, per-placement recipe validation or inventory-driven catalogue rebuild.
   - Planned focused commands after code exists: dotnet build .\VanillaExpanded\VanillaExpanded.csproj --no-restore; dotnet test .\VanillaExpanded.Tests\VanillaExpanded.Tests.csproj --no-restore --filter "Category=Unit&FullyQualifiedName~CraftingMenu"; corresponding Category=E2E feature filter when actual integration tests exist. Use the existing Cake build in Phase 7. Missing dependencies may be restored only after a command reports that requirement.
   - Observed validation: read-only source/decompilation inspection only. No build/test execution is needed to validate these documentation-only findings; any later execution must use the required separate verification subagent. These notes do not satisfy Phase 4 transfer tests or Phase 7 live evidence.

### Selected client-side operation and later verification

The user rejected the custom server-side batch and server-owned catalogue. Neither is part of the selected architecture. Catalogue construction and transfer planning run on the client; execution uses native inventory APIs and their existing synchronization rather than feature-specific server messages.

Source verification of the available client operations:

- [IPlayerInventoryManager.cs](../../../vsapi/Common/Entity/Player/IPlayerInventoryManager.cs), lines 155-178, exposes TryTransferTo(sourceSlot, targetSlot, ref op) and TryTransferAway(sourceSlot, ref op, onlyPlayerInventory, ...). TryTransferAway returns an object[] of generated native packets; that alone does not establish a single atomic arrangement.
- [IInventoryNetworkUtil.cs](../../../vsapi/Common/Inventory/IInventoryNetworkUtil.cs) exposes GetActivateSlotPacket, GetFlipSlotsPacket, HandleClientPacket, SendInventoryRollback, and PauseInventoryUpdates. The rollback interface identifies one client packet, not an entire multi-move plan. No verified contract makes PauseInventoryUpdates a mutation queue, flush, acknowledgement barrier, or transaction; the selected mechanism does not depend on it.
- Use the native operations to enforce transfers, merges, bag persistence, output recalculation, and normal synchronization. Do not assign live Itemstack/Attributes references, replace slot objects, manually construct crafting output, or suppress native callbacks to simulate a transaction.
- A bulk client action orchestrates native moves after complete local preflight. The user approved normal native partial progress: on an observed native refusal, stop issuing new moves, retain accepted transfers, let already-submitted packets resolve natively, reconcile, and explain the interruption. There is no accepted-prefix or cancellation guarantee for already-in-flight packets. Do not restore an old snapshot or automatically replay accepted moves. Local preflight refusals remain unchanged-state operations.
- The client-loaded World.GridRecipes and ResolvedIngredients are the catalogue source. Local recipe identity supports stable selection/order only; it is not a new server token. Existing native recipe loading/synchronization and crafting output matching remain responsible for actual craftability.
- Planned evidence: focused native-operation tests for preflight, excluded slots, planned destination acceptance, heat restrictions, partial/stale refusal, overlap suppression, and reconciliation; live multiplayer checks remain required later.

Selected native lifecycle/execution sequence:

1. Reuse the already-open player crafting inventory and carried inventories. [IInventory.cs](../../../vsapi/Common/Inventory/IInventory.cs), Open/Close contract (lines 64-79), and InventoryBase.Open/Close (lines 845-870) return lifecycle packet objects through InvNetworkUtil.DidOpen/DidClose. The caller sends those packets; they do not commit a list of arbitrary dirty-slot edits.
2. Plan the full arrangement locally, including return capacity, native acceptance/merge constraints, eligible slots, and heat restrictions. If local preflight fails, perform no mutations and send no transfer packets.
3. On the client owning thread, revalidate the plan and use player.InventoryManager.TryTransferTo(source, target, ref moveOperation) for each explicit planned move. It applies the normal native operation and returns its native packet. TryTransferAway can supply native shift-style destinations only where that behavior matches the eligible-destination contract; an unrestricted transfer-away must not route into excluded slots/containers.
4. Send every non-null returned packet through capi.Network.SendPacketClient(object), declared in [IClientNetworkAPI.cs](../../../vsapi/Client/API/IClientNetworkAPI.cs), line 197. A TryTransferAway result may contain multiple packets: send each through the same normal API. Do not wrap them in a custom feature message or imply that the group is atomically accepted.
5. Hold a local execution guard during preflight/revalidation and dispatch, releasing it when that local action ends; the user explicitly approved allowing another click while native reconciliation remains pending. Observe native inventory updates and refresh shared previews from the resulting state. Never invent a positive batch acknowledgement or blindly resubmit on mismatch. On detected interruption, stop unsent work and reconcile the native result, including any in-flight operations.
6. Leave parent-owned inventories open. Their existing GUI lifecycle sends the normal Close packet when the panel actually closes; do not force that close to synchronize placement. Direct client Itemstack assignment plus MarkDirty only dirties local display/model state: IInventory.MarkSlotDirty (lines 125-132) documents that server dirtying sends contents, while client dirtying updates local rendering. It is not a client-to-server mutation/flush protocol.

Native observation and guard boundaries:

- Installed Vintagestory.Common.InventoryNetworkUtil.UpdateFromPacket overloads accept IWorldAccessor plus Packet_InventoryContents, Packet_InventoryUpdate, or Packet_InventoryDoubleUpdate; UpdateSlotStack applies the native slot replacement. PlayerInventoryNetworkUtil and CraftingInventoryNetworkUtil also have update paths. These are concrete authoritative-update observation points, not feature handlers or batch results.
- The user superseded the earlier packet-observation design: do not patch native packet-processing entry points or distinguish local mutations from server-applied inventory changes. Subscribe to eligible native SlotModified events and schedule a coalesced preview invalidation from current client inventory. The game owns authoritative synchronization; no post-dispatch projection histories, inferred corrections or server-outcome notifications belong in the feature.
- A locally observed native transfer refusal or competing inventory change during dispatch stops unsent work and reports interruption. Already-sent operations resolve normally. After dispatch, inventory changes only invalidate previews; do not infer server acceptance/rejection or replay moves.
- No public batch acknowledgement, pending-transfer idle state, cancellation API, or native completion event was found on the inspected manager/network utility types. The local execution guard therefore ends with local execution, not with a timeout, inferred acknowledgement, or presumed server acceptance. This matches the user's explicit guard choice. Later updates remain observable after release; panel/world close removes feature state/subscriptions without undoing native moves.
- Phase 4 tests cover local operation failure, native partial progress, ordinary inventory notifications, coalescing, a second click while synchronization remains pending, and lifecycle teardown. No final arrangement is promised merely because all packets were sent. The installed crafting-grid full-contents path bypasses SlotModified and MarkedDirty; retain an explicit regression and Phase 5 integration obligation instead of claiming native events cover that path.

The Phase 1 client operation and failure/guard contract were established by source inspection and independent audit, not runtime transfers. Later implementation evidence appears under Phase 4; the user's native-event simplification supersedes the earlier requirement for packet-origin observation. Live multiplayer checks remain Phase 7 obligations.

Completion criteria:

    ✔ Source-backed integration decisions and all blocking questions above are recorded before their consumers begin.
    ✔ The native client bulk mechanism and its safety/reconciliation contract are verified; no custom server transaction or unproved atomicity is substituted.
    ✔ Intended feature/test locations and the focused validation commands are identified without implementing this plan during its creation.

## Phase 2: Build the eligible catalogue and recipe-selection model

Dependencies: Phase 1.
Outcome: eligible outputs and alternatives can be enumerated, searched, and selected independently of the GUI.

    ✔ Construct the catalogue when loaded recipes become available.
        ✔ Include enabled grid recipes available to the player, compatible mod recipes, grid-fit constraints, and native visibility/access rules.
        ✔ Group recipes behind one tile per distinct resolved output, preserving materially distinguishing attributes.
        ✔ Preserve each alternative's output quantity, identity, ingredients, native constraints, and support status.
        ✔ Represent dynamic outputs honestly using the Phase 1 decisions; expose unsupported alternatives rather than hiding them.
    ✔ Expose opening-time/fresh-snapshot candidate discovery without an incremental inventory cache.
        ✔ Union recipes accepting any unique inventory stack through the native ingredient index; retain attribute-sensitive variants and ignore sufficiency at this broad stage.
        ✔ Restrict candidates to eligible catalogue entries, deduplicate recipes, and conservatively handle unindexed/custom/zero-ingredient recipes.
        ✔ Retain the complete searchable catalogue separately; leave exact allocation and inventory capture to Phase 3 and event wiring to Phase 5.
    ✔ Implement normalized localized-name search and secondary item-code matching.
        ✔ Ignore case, trim whitespace, require all search words to match, and preserve relevance ordering within filtered results.
        ✔ Return the full catalogue for empty queries and an explicit empty-result state when nothing matches.
    ✔ Model automatic and explicit alternative selection.
        ✔ Choose the highest-relevance supported alternative once Phase 3 scores are available; use stable recipe identifiers when none are supported.
        ✔ Preserve explicit selection while the tile remains selected, including an unsupported selection when supported alternatives exist.
        ✔ Restore automatic choice on the agreed release interaction.
        ✔ Expose one selected-recipe state for output quantity, availability, tooltip, relevance, and placement.
    ✔ Add focused unit coverage for eligibility, output-attribute grouping, alternative identity/selection, dynamic previews, unsupported discoverability, and search semantics.
        ✔ Use deterministic allocation-result fixtures for ranking consumers until Phase 3 is integrated; do not invent runtime scores for unsupported recipes.

Completion criteria:

    ✔ Catalogue/search tests cover enabled/disabled recipes, excluded processes, grid fit, output variants, mod alternatives, and empty/multiword/localized queries.
    ✔ Every eligible resolved output and unsupported preview is discoverable under its documented representation.
    ✔ Selection and support states are explicit and cannot silently make an unsupported alternative fillable.

### Phase 2 traceability and verification

Controlling sources: the complete [proposal](./CraftingMenuProposal.md), especially Scope, Outputs and alternative recipes, Relevance and availability, and Catalogue lifecycle and performance; Phase 1's native integration findings; [repository instructions](../.github/copilot-instructions.md) and [project.todo](./project.todo) conventions. The user's latest refresh-policy answer is full recomputation on inventory changes, not an opening-only frozen view.

- Catalogue/filter/grouping tasks -> proposal Scope and Outputs -> `CraftingCatalogue.Build` accepts loaded native recipe objects, enforces grid-only/enabled/visible/grid-fit filters, and requires an explicit player-access predicate. No permissive default is supplied. The caller must provide verified native access policy at client integration; the current stage has no player/GUI wiring. Runtime metadata comes from ResolvedIngredients; output grouping compares native attribute trees without ignoring subtrees or quantity-dependent alternative differences.
- Candidate tasks -> proposal Catalogue lifecycle and performance -> `FindCandidates` creates fresh local stack snapshots and a candidate union using World.FastSearchRecipesByIngredient. Native ingredient lookup code: [CraftingRecipeIngredient.cs](../../../vsapi/Common/Crafting/CraftingRecipeIngredient.cs), AddToFastSearchRecipes/SatisfiesAsIngredient/FastSearchCraftingRecipeIngredient; native usage: [CollectibleBehaviorHandbookTextAndExtraInfo.cs](../../../vssurvivalmod/Systems/Handbook/CollectibleBehaviorHandbookTextAndExtraInfo.cs), addIngredientForInfo. Persistent and temporary attributes use type-preserving binary per-call identities; custom ingredient types are detected before native Clone can erase their subclass. Broad matches ignore sufficiency, unindexed native predicates have a fallback, and exceptional/zero-ingredient cases remain candidates. No inventory results persist between calls.
- Search/selection tasks -> proposal Outputs, Player experience and Relevance -> `CraftingCatalogueView` exposes a single selected recipe/evaluation, all-word localized-name/code search, deterministic ordering, explicit alternative pinning, and Auto/deselection release. Complete score replacement preserves explicit pins but discards old scores.
- Support/preview tasks -> proposal unsupported/dynamic baseline -> unresolved data cannot become fillable through a supplied score. Output-generation/consumption callbacks are never invoked; previews are copied, dynamic/custom previews are marked generic, and absent Phase 3 results are explicitly unknown with localized reasons, not fabricated numeric zero. Phase 3 supplies verified support/allocation results; fixtures exist only in tests.
- Focused evidence: `VanillaExpanded.Tests/Unit/CraftingMenu` covers filtering, variants, duplicate identity, unsupported discovery, dynamic previews, candidate recomputation, search, ranking and selection. Separate verification ran `dotnet test .\VanillaExpanded.Tests\VanillaExpanded.Tests.csproj --no-restore --filter "Category=Unit&FullyQualifiedName~CraftingMenu"` against net10.0/VSTest: 40 passed, 0 failed, 0 skipped. The command built production/test projects; 34 unrelated existing warnings were observed, with none in CraftingMenu. Staged/unstaged whitespace checks and localization JSON parsing passed.
- Second review identified native ItemStack.Clone omitting temporary attributes; explicit temporary snapshots and regression coverage were added. The independent completion audit subsequently identified JSON attribute-type conflation and ingredient subclass loss through native Clone; both are corrected with binary identities/original-type detection and dedicated regressions. Independent affected-requirement recheck confirmed both fixes and found no remaining completion blockers. Phase 2 is complete.
- GUI opening/change subscriptions, actual eligible-inventory capture, native access-policy wiring, and exact matching/allocation are intentionally later-phase consumers, not claimed runtime behavior here. No performance threshold, live-client behavior, placement, or package validation is inferred from this unit evidence.

## Phase 3: Implement shared allocation, relevance, and placement objectives

Dependencies: Phases 1 and 2.
Outcome: deterministic inventory snapshots produce consistent availability, missing requirements, and placement allocations.

    ✔ Build eligible inventory snapshots using the verified source list.
        ✔ Count existing grid contents once and retain stable inventory/slot identity.
        ✔ Read game-owned mutable data only on its owning thread; expose safe snapshots to any deferred work.
    ✔ Allocate against the complete recipe through native matching semantics or verified supported adapters.
        ✔ Preserve wildcards, restricted alternatives, repeated requirements, attributes, tool presence, durability, and relationships between choices.
        ✔ Resolve overlapping alternatives globally; never let a flexible requirement consume the only item usable by a restricted requirement.
        ✔ Prevent double allocation of physical items and incompatible-stack merging; each grid position holds one compatible stack.
        ✔ Preserve tools/non-consumed requirements as such, rather than ordinary consumable quantities.
        ✔ Return explicit unsupported reasons for exceptional semantics without a supported adapter.
    ✔ Derive one-execution coverage, relevance, availability, and missing explanations from shared results.
        ✔ Compute A/R from maximally allocatable simultaneous ordinary units; a valid presence-only tool contributes one required unit.
        ✔ Require a complete valid allocation for availability; distinguish this from transfer feasibility.
        ✔ Aggregate identical missing requirements only when constraints are preserved; describe alternatives as choices, not separate mandatory ingredients.
        ✔ Keep unsupported relevance unknown and avoid fabricated deficits.
    ✔ Implement deterministic ranking and integrate it with Phase 2 selection/search.
        ✔ Sort satisfiable recipes first, then incomplete supported recipes by descending relevance.
        ✔ Break ties by fewer missing units, localized output name, then stable output/recipe identifier.
        ✔ Sort selected unsupported recipes last by localized output name and stable identifiers, even when another alternative is supported.
    ✔ Produce ordinary-click allocations for one execution.
        ✔ Preserve shaped layouts and choose a stable legal shapeless layout.
        ✔ Populate partial quantities and leave wholly missing requirements empty without fabricating inventory stacks.
    ✔ Produce shift-click allocations by joint lexicographic optimization.
        ✔ First maximize single-execution coverage, spreading scarce shared ingredients across required positions.
        ✔ Next maximize supported complete executions under per-position requirements and slot limits.
        ✔ Finally place remaining compatible surplus without reducing earlier optima; prefer least-filled equivalent positions with stable grid-position ties.
        ✔ Reconsider preliminary variants when later objectives improve without weakening earlier objectives; use inventory order only among equally optimal choices.
        ✔ Continue filling available positions when another ingredient is entirely missing; retain permitted surplus for later manual crafting.
        ✔ Do not auto-replace tools or claim crafting beyond their actual durability.
    ✔ Add focused unit tests under VanillaExpanded.Tests/Unit/{Feature}, marked Category=Unit, reusing existing mocks and real InventoryGeneric where appropriate.
        ✔ Prove four boards plus two sticks with three boards and one stick scores 4/6, missing one of each; board surplus cannot compensate for missing sticks.
        ✔ Prove two repeated one-unit positions receive 10/10 from 20 units and 64/63 from 127 units with 64-unit limits.
        ✔ Prove one early oak board does not lock a position when 64 pine boards improve batch size at equal first-execution coverage.
        ✔ Cover constrained-overlap allocation, incompatible variants, non-stackable tools, insufficient durability, missing ingredients, shaped/shapeless layouts, and stable ties.
        ✔ Check conservation, slot limits, no double counting, ranking/tooltip agreement, and maximality against exhaustive feasible allocations for small bounded fixtures.

Completion criteria:

    ✔ Focused allocation/ranking tests pass, including the proposal's numerical examples and constrained-variant cases.
    ✔ Preview, missing lists, availability, and placement consume shared semantic results rather than separate matching implementations.
    ✔ Shift-click satisfies each objective before tie-breaking, including incomplete recipes; unsupported recipes cannot produce actionable allocations.

### Phase 3 traceability and verification

Controlling sources consulted: the complete [proposal](./CraftingMenuProposal.md), especially Inventory and ingredient matching, Relevance and availability, both placement sections, Missing-ingredient tooltip, and Catalogue lifecycle and performance; Phase 1's verified sources/decisions and Phase 2's catalogue contracts above; [repository instructions](../.github/copilot-instructions.md) and [project.todo](./project.todo). Phase 3 is complete after focused verification, second review, and the independent completion audit, including affected-requirement rechecks (2026-10-28).

- Snapshot tasks -> proposal Inventory and ingredient matching / Catalogue lifecycle -> `CraftingInventorySnapshot.Capture` reads only verified player-owned hotbar 0-9, backpack contents from 4, and crafting inputs, with native access/take checks, stable inventory/slot addresses, defensive persistent/temporary attribute copies, and an owning-thread guard. Unknown inventory adapters/topologies are explicit errors. Deferred native matching is not enabled: collectible references and native predicates remain owning-thread-only.
- Matching/support tasks -> proposal native authority / exceptional-adapter baseline -> `CraftingAllocator` uses native `SatisfiesAsIngredient`, `MatchesForCrafting`, consume properties and remaining durability. The bounded adapter supports independent base grid/ingredient semantics, including native average-durability output with an honest generic preview (no output attributes are manufactured). Custom recipe/ingredient relationships, copied/merged attributes, freshness-transforming outputs, exceptional non-consumed quantities, and attribute-transforming merges remain explicitly unsupported, never assigned fake deficits. The independent-policy input handles native winner ambiguity, including incomplete layouts. Collectible callbacks and recipe-event subscriptions are not compatibility gates; the reflection-based restrictions originally recorded for this phase were removed by the user-approved follow-up below.
- Native source parity -> [CraftingRecipeIngredient.cs](../../../vsapi/Common/Crafting/CraftingRecipeIngredient.cs), SatisfiesAsIngredient/GetConsumeProperties; [Collectible.cs](../../../vsapi/Common/Collectible/Collectible.cs), MatchesForCrafting/GetRemainingDurability/GetMergableQuantity; [RecipeBase.cs](../../../vsapi/Common/Crafting/RecipeBase.cs), shaped/shapeless matching; [GridRecipe.cs](../../../vsapi/Common/Crafting/GridRecipe.cs), Matches and its two native event gates. Installed 1.22.7.0 `InventoryCraftingGrid.FindMatchingRecipe` was rechecked: native candidate order is input-slot then ingredient-index order, deduplicated and enabled-only, followed by shaped-before-shapeless matching. It is not simply registry order. Complete planned layouts validate this winner without generating or consuming output.
- Allocation/relevance/placement tasks -> proposal joint objectives -> one solver enumerates compatible variant assignments globally (canonicalizing exchangeable positions), computes simultaneous single-execution coverage, batch limits including tool durability/reserved tools, then compatible surplus. Per-group bounded water-filling spreads scarce units and surplus, with grid-position ties. Separate ordinary/shift plans retain exact source contributions; both share requirement descriptors and one-execution missing/evaluation data. Missing entries retain per-position native choice constraints instead of unsafe aggregation.
- Selection/ranking tasks -> proposal single selected-recipe state -> `CraftingCatalogueView.ReplaceAllocations` supplies the exact shared evaluation and source-backed placement result to Phase 2 ordering/search/pinning. Fresh candidate union controls expensive source matching; verified independent non-candidates are evaluated against an empty supply, while unknown/custom cases stay unsupported. No inventory results are retained across computations.
- Focused evidence planned/implemented in `CraftingAllocationTests` and `CraftingInventorySnapshotTests`: numerical examples, global constrained overlaps, incompatible variants/attributes, allowed/skipped wildcards, tool presence/durability, shaped holes/shapeless layout, native events/winner ordering, fresh candidate recomputation, shared ranking/missing/plans, stable ties, source conservation, snapshot eligibility/ownership and slot limits. The bounded exhaustive oracle independently enumerates every two-position variant/count allocation for stock 0-3, demand 1-2, and all nonempty two-variant acceptance sets.
- Second review checked the implementation against every mapped requirement and added regressions/fixes for exchangeable-position balancing before inventory ties, aliased physical stack rejection, accurate insufficient-durability explanations, finite maximum durability versus unbounded presence, and huge source quantities bounded by usable grid capacity. The historical independent audit also introduced rejection of custom `DamageItem`/`DestroyItem` overrides; the user subsequently rejected that compatibility gate. The updated source-only custom-damage regression accepts placement and verifies allocation does not invoke durability mutation. The original independent verdict covered its original implementation, not this later removal.
- Separate exact-revision verification ran `dotnet test .\VanillaExpanded.Tests\VanillaExpanded.Tests.csproj --no-restore --filter "Category=Unit&FullyQualifiedName~CraftingMenu"`: 73 passed, 0 failed, 0 skipped (net10.0/VSTest); production/test compilation succeeded with 34 existing unrelated warnings and none in CraftingMenu. Whitespace checks and localization JSON parsing passed. This includes the negative native durability-cost overflow regression added during the final support-boundary recheck.
- Transfer preflight/execution, actual GUI/event/access-policy integration, deferred computation, live-client compatibility and performance thresholds remain later phases. Allocations do not mutate inventory, consume ingredients, generate output, send packets, or promise transfer feasibility.

## Phase 4: Implement native client bulk transfer planning and execution

Dependencies: Phases 1 and 3.
Outcome: supported allocations can safely rearrange the grid without crafting or losing items.

    ✔ Plan reuse, repositioning, and return of existing grid contents before execution.
        ✔ Validate destination acceptance, native restrictions, compatible merges, quantities, slot limits, and carried-storage capacity for every displaced item.
        ✔ Keep ingredient sufficiency independent from rearrangement capacity; report the latter as a placement failure.
        ✔ Leave the original grid unchanged when preflight finds that displaced items cannot be returned; never drop items automatically.
        ✔ Reject no-usable-ingredient actions with an explicit reason rather than clearing an unrelated grid.
    ✔ Implement the Phase 1 client-side bulk mechanism using native inventory operations and standard synchronization.
        ✔ Use the selected catalogue recipe and revalidate supported allocation, eligible sources, destinations, quantities, and current client inventory state before submission; recipe definitions and eligibility are immutable during the loaded world's catalogue lifetime.
        ✔ Reject or recompute locally stale/unsafe plans before execution; on observed native refusal stop unsent work, retain accepted transfers, let in-flight operations resolve natively, and reconcile without snapshot restoration or blind replay.
        ✔ Prevent duplication, loss, unauthorized access, output consumption, and stack/attribute writes bypassing native inventory APIs.
        ✔ Guard overlapping local execution, allow another click after dispatch as approved, and report locally observed native failure without inferring server outcomes or adding custom mod server handlers/packets.
    ✔ Apply ordinary and shift-click plans through the same transfer-safety boundary.
        ✔ Invalidate shared previews through coalesced native inventory notifications and read current inventory, without claiming server-confirmed placement success.
        ✔ Leave existing output-taking and manual crafting code paths intact.
    ✔ Add focused transfer unit and integration/E2E tests, using existing network mocks and Category=Unit or Category=E2E appropriately.
        ✔ Cover grid reuse without double counting, full storage, incompatible destinations, mid-plan refusal, and zero usable ingredients.
        ✔ Cover stale inventories, immutable-catalogue reuse, invalid or excluded source slots, invalid quantities/destinations, duplicate/reentrant local actions, competing updates, and another click during native reconciliation.
        ✔ Treat native slot changes as preview invalidations regardless of origin, coalesce refreshes, and leave authoritative synchronization to the game without packet-processing hooks or correction histories.
        ✔ Assert exact unchanged state on local preflight refusal, and accepted-transfer preservation plus native reconciliation on later refusal, including already-in-flight packets; assert conservation and no automatic crafting/output-taking.

Completion criteria:

    ✔ Automated tests demonstrate the agreed preflight, native execution, and failure/reconciliation contract; no unverified all-or-nothing claim is inferred from packet batching.
    ✔ Both click modes use the same validated transfer boundary with explicit error results.
    ✔ Client placement uses standard native operations/synchronization without custom server transactions or direct stack edits, and native crafting remains separate.

### Phase 4 working evidence and traceability (2026-10-28)

Status: the original implementation passed second review and independent full/affected-requirement audits. The user subsequently approved removal of immutable-recipe defenses and packet-level reconciliation tracking. The native-event design supersedes those parts of the audited baseline; its verification is recorded below. The full-contents refresh gap left by this phase is now resolved by the Phase 5 GUI lifecycle bridge and installed-native regression.

Controlling sources consulted: this phase and its inherited proposal, Phase 1's selected native operation/failure contract and source baseline, Phase 3's source-backed allocation boundary, repository instructions, and project.todo conventions. Native inventory source is the read-only 1.22.7 checkout identified in Phase 1; concrete manager/network methods were rechecked in installed VintagestoryLib.dll 1.22.7.0. No native sources were modified.

- Planning/reuse/return tasks -> proposal "Inventory and ingredient matching", "Ordinary click placement", and "Shift-click placement"; Phase 1 inventory allowlist; ItemSlot.CanTake/CanHold/CanTakeFrom, InventoryBase.CanContain/DropSlotIfHot, and BagInventory.ItemSlotBagContent -> CraftingTransferPlanner validates source-backed allocations, quantities, input-only targets, exact attributes, native detached-slot acceptance, locks, storage flags/tags, capacities and heat before any live operation. It retains existing matching inputs, directly repositions excess, fills targets to free carried slots, returns displaced contents, and can use a vacant input as a native-transfer buffer. A repeated projected layout or lack of a safe move is an explicit placement-capacity refusal, not evidence of ingredient insufficiency or a reason to drop items. Unknown movement/merge/transition/bag semantics and temporary metadata that native whole-stack splitting would discard are explicitly unsafe rather than approximated. Unsupported transfer feasibility does not change ingredient relevance.
- Submission/guard tasks -> proposal "Proposed implementation boundaries" and Phase 1 native dispatch sequence; IPlayerInventoryManager.TryTransferTo and IClientNetworkAPI.SendPacketClient -> CraftingTransferCoordinator uses the supplied selected catalogue recipe, captures fresh eligible stock and recomputes allocation for either mode. Recipe definitions and eligibility are trusted for the catalogue lifetime; lifecycle cleanup owns disposal on parent/world close. Per-placement definition serialization, selection/registry membership scans and access-predicate callbacks were removed at the user's direction as unnecessary defenses against immutable state. Full detached preflight and immediate native topology/state/permission revalidation precede dispatch. Only native TryTransferTo changes live slots; every returned packet, including a partial native move, is sent normally. The local guard covers preparation and dispatch only. A refusal or competing change stops unsent moves, retains accepted moves, and never restores, cancels, replays, closes inventories to flush, or takes output.
- Inventory refresh tasks -> proposal's native-event boundary and Phase 1 source evidence -> CraftingTransferCoordinator subscribes to eligible hotbar/backpack/grid SlotModified events and queues one main-thread InventoryChanged invalidation for a burst. The preview consumer reads fresh inventory when that callback runs, regardless of whether changes originated locally or from synchronization. Disposal/parent close/LeaveWorld remove subscriptions and suppress queued callbacks. No packet patches, observation ModSystem, post-dispatch histories, authoritative-change event or server-correction notification remain. Native preflight/dispatch checks still detect competing local changes and preserve partial progress. The concrete crafting-grid contents regression confirms both SlotModified and MarkedDirty are bypassed while the native output DirtySlots flag is set; no GUI refresh adapter is claimed for that path yet.
- Focused test tasks -> proposal acceptance criteria and Phase 1 transfer evidence obligations -> CraftingTransferTests uses the established AllocationFixture, real InventoryGeneric slots/native player manager, and a Moq IClientNetworkAPI (the existing MockClientNetworkChannel is for custom feature messages, which this operation must not introduce). CraftingNativeTransferTests exercises installed native transfers and ordinary slot notifications from native synchronization, without Harmony patches. Tests cover local partial refusal, coalesced local/server-origin changes without classification, paused updates, native contents/double updates, cleanup and the explicit concrete crafting-grid full-contents notification gap. These are controlled automated integration/E2E fixtures, not a running client/server or live multiplayer claim.
- Second review checks include complete-plan conservation, source eligibility, inventory mutation, native capacity/restrictions, full-storage buffering, partial native refusal/packet preservation, output non-consumption, reentrancy, another click before settlement, coalesced native-event invalidation and teardown. Live GUI binding, policy selection, preview rendering and multiplayer validation remain Phases 5-7.
- The historical independent audit introduced reflection-based rejection of native modification, transition, behavior-discovery and temperature overrides. That compatibility gate was subsequently rejected by the user and removed, not cached or replaced by a type whitelist. Twenty-four updated regressions cover six forwarding overrides as an ingredient or displaced stack in both modes, verifying successful native dispatch rather than blanket refusal. Actual stack transition, bag, temperature, temporary-attribute, slot, merge and capacity restrictions remain. The original audit is historical evidence, not an audit of this follow-up.
- Separate feature verification at the completion audit passed 143 tests. After the user's immutable-recipe cleanup, `dotnet test .\VanillaExpanded.Tests\VanillaExpanded.Tests.csproj --no-restore --filter "FullyQualifiedName~CraftingMenu"` passed 137 tests, 0 failed, 0 skipped (net10.0/VSTest), including both Unit and controlled E2E cases. Eight hypothetical recipe-mutation/selection-staleness cases were removed and two ordinary/shift regressions assert that placement never queries the recipe registry. Production/test compilation succeeded; the warning baseline remains 34 unrelated warnings and no CraftingMenu warnings. `git diff --check` and localization JSON parsing passed. A native-grid fixture initialization failure was fixed using the established null-API-then-initialize pattern; it was a fixture problem, not omitted evidence.
- Current verification after the user-approved native-notification cleanup: the same focused command passed 139 tests, 0 failed, 0 skipped; production/test compilation, whitespace validation and localization JSON parsing passed. A targeted source/test search confirmed no packet-observation class, authoritative-change event, correction-history state or correction notification remains. Refresh callbacks are coalesced and canceled on close/disposal; existing transfer safety and native in-flight processing remain intact. Complete live preview coverage for crafting-grid full contents is not claimed.

## Phase 5: Integrate the popout and consistent player interactions

Dependencies: Phases 2, 3, and 4.
Outcome: the actual crafting panel exposes searchable previews and safe placement actions.

    ✔ Add the crafting-menu toggle beside the grid with attachment, visibility, and event wiring kept narrow.
