    ✔ Selected recipe, icon quantity, tooltip, score, and placement agree before and after updates.
    ✔ Automated interaction-state tests pass; live layout/input checks remain explicitly pending until Phase 7.

### Phase 5 working evidence and traceability (2026-10-28)

Status: the original Phase 5 implementation completed second review, delegated verification and independent full/affected-requirement completion audits. The full audit found a misplaced shapeless-competition loop and omitted tag explanations; both were fixed, covered by regressions, and independently rechecked as resolved. The subsequent user-requested native-GUI abstraction refactor and its separate verification are recorded below; the earlier audits do not cover that refactor. This is not a live-client, performance or packaging acceptance claim.

Controlling sources consulted: inherited proposal (Scope, Player experience, Outputs and alternative recipes, Relevance and availability, Missing-ingredient tooltip, both placement modes, implementation boundaries and catalogue lifecycle), Phase 1 native integration decisions, Phase 2 catalogue/selection, Phase 3 allocation/support policy, Phase 4 native transfer/event contract, repository instructions and project.todo conventions. Native sources remain read-only at the Phase 1 revisions; installed concrete GUI/event/inventory methods were inspected in 1.22.7.0.

- Toggle/attachment/rendering tasks -> proposal Player experience and Phase 1 GUI traceability -> CraftingMenuDialogPatch observes only ComposeSurvivalInvDialog and receives its actual newly built composer. CraftingMenuSystem adds an idempotently keyed native button directly to the already-composed GUI, recomposes, and leaves native inputs/output untouched. GuiDialogCraftingMenu uses parent-relative ElementBounds, composer child scopes and native clipping, closes/disposes on parent/world closure or composer replacement, and recomposes on size/scale changes. CraftingMenuLayout retains only attachment policy: first try the parent's right/left side, then free space outside the entire input/output bounding rectangle. There is no parallel rectangle model. Native-bounds tests cover parent movement/padding, representative 800x600, 1280x720 and 1920x1080 viewports and scales 1, 1.5 and 2. A physically impossible viewport produces an explicit resize/reduce-scale refusal rather than covering the grid. Actual visual usability is not inferred from geometry.
- Shared selection/inspection/input tasks -> proposal Outputs, Relevance, Missing tooltip and both placement modes -> CraftingMenuSession wires real catalogue candidate discovery and Phase 3 allocations, not score fixtures. Hover/pressed/keyboard interaction freezes visible order and automatic recipe identity while replacing that recipe's current allocation/deficits. Leaving the interaction commits ordering. Alternative arrows pin a recipe, Auto releases it, and selecting another tile releases the old pin. Native search input/scrollbar, GuiElementContainer and image-only GuiElementTextButton subclasses own mouse capture, hit testing, button visuals, focus and Tab traversal. The grid adapter retains bounded row recycling, virtual-row focus navigation, arrows, Space/Shift activation and session binding. Native composer clips and owns the tooltip; there is no custom scissor or render-loop pointer polling. Only visible tiles use native RenderItemstackToGui. Markers !/?/+ supplement greying. Tooltip ingredients, first-execution deficits, allowed/skipped choices, tool durability reasons, generic-preview disclaimer and action hints all use the same selected recipe. Unsupported entries remain inspectable and selectable without dispatch.
- Production eligibility/support binding -> proposal Scope and Phase 1 recipe source evidence -> CraftingClientPolicy uses CharacterSystem.HasTrait, the native access rule, when building the catalogue. It does not inspect private recipe-event subscriptions or certify their declaring types; native matching invokes event rules normally. Native-winner ambiguity is conservatively unsupported, including padded layouts and native shapeless merging across different declared ingredient counts. This disjointness check does not choose a winner or override native Matches; the allocator retains complete-layout native validation. Unknown custom recipe classes cannot certify ordinary recipes they might override. Recipe-output ambiguity and explicit exceptional recipe semantics remain separate from the removed collectible/event compatibility gates.
- Refresh/placement tasks -> proposal Catalogue lifecycle and Phase 4 notifications/native synchronization -> native SlotModified and the new GuiElementCraftingRefresh coalesce through the existing coordinator queue. The custom element's earlier DrawOrder observes output DirtySlots[9] in PostRenderInteractiveElements before the native output element consumes it. It does not patch rendering/packets, consume flags or run allocation per frame. An installed-native contents/update/finalization regression proves this previously documented gap is covered. Clicks still capture fresh inventory through the Phase 4 coordinator even with frozen previews; standard packets, local guard, partial progress and native output-taking remain unchanged. Actual registry rebuild wiring and longer-lived catalogue caching remain the explicitly assigned Phase 6 work.
- Automated interaction tasks -> proposal acceptance criteria -> CraftingMenuInteractionTests covers real allocation/dispatch for both inputs, fresh-state refusal while ordering is frozen, automatic/explicit alternative stability, unknown-state refusal, every first-execution deficit and wildcard choices, empty search, keyboard/mouse routing, native scrollbar behavior, access/event policy and representative geometry. The installed-native GUI refresh regression remains Category=E2E, not live multiplayer evidence. Tests reference the installed cairo-sharp assembly and copy the installed Windows native Cairo library into test output for native button/font construction; no PATH workaround is required. Headless scrollbar tests bypass only texture generation, not input/state logic. GUI-scale changes are isolated in a nonparallel test collection and restored.

Observed delegated verification: dotnet test .\VanillaExpanded.Tests\VanillaExpanded.Tests.csproj --no-restore --filter "FullyQualifiedName~CraftingMenu" passed 163 tests, 0 failed, 0 skipped; production/test compilation succeeded with 34 unrelated existing warnings and no CraftingMenu warnings. git diff --check and localization JSON parsing passed. Second review checked the source requirements and production wiring, including already-composed native button insertion, disposal/scale/minimize handling, native shapeless merging, scrollbar/focus state and stationary-pointer keyboard precedence. Affected review additionally verified the shapeless-only registry scan and localized tag explanations against ComplexTagCondition.Matches and ITagRegistry.SlowEnumerateTagNames: outer AND/OR, inner any/all, forbidden tags and empty-required choices are explicit in both ingredients and deficits. Tag-name lookup uses the native client registry; tests check native positive/negative matching examples. Independent audit and affected recheck concluded Phase 5 fully satisfied. Missing-position outlines are optional and omitted; no preview item is installed in any inventory. No separate audit report file is required.

Separate live-client cases, intentionally pending Phase 7: toggle/recompose after changing backpack rows or game mode; follow a moved parent and resize/minimize/restore; inspect both attachment sides at supported UI scales and the smallest practical viewport; browse/scroll a long catalogue; Tab/arrow/search focus and mouse/keyboard tooltips; alternatives including unsupported entries and Auto release; concurrent native single/double/full-contents updates with a stationary pointer; ordinary/Shift placement followed by manual output-taking; parent/world closure during queued refresh. Live multiplayer, packaging, representative timings/thresholds and broad compatibility are not claimed here.

Native-GUI follow-up verification: the focused CraftingMenu command above passed 165 tests, 0 failed, 0 skipped, with normal inherited PATH, successful production/test compilation and no CraftingMenu warnings (34 unrelated existing warnings). Added regressions prove a 256-recipe list uses six recycled native buttons in a 96x72 viewport; forward/reverse Tab traverses virtual rows and enters/exits both ends correctly; native clipping rejects overscan clicks; mouse press/release remains bound to one recipe during refresh, attempted scrolling and partially visible-row focus; keyboard activation restores focused-tile inspection without stationary-pointer polling. Existing allocation, native transfer and full-contents refresh coverage still passes. No new independent completion audit or live-client rendering run was performed for this refactor. Phase 6 lifecycle/performance work and Phase 7 live acceptance remain pending.

## Phase 6: Complete catalogue/resource lifecycle and bounded rendering

Dependencies: Phase 5.
Outcome: the integrated feature has bounded rendering work and correct world/panel cleanup. User-approved scope amendments (2026-10-29): catalogue rebuilds occur only at world loading because recipes are immutable while loaded; vanilla/heavily modded benchmarks and numerical thresholds are not required.

    ✔ Build a catalogue for each loaded world and clear obsolete catalogue/selection/results on world exit; do not observe in-world recipe-registry changes.
        ✔ Apply the documented immutable visibility/access/grid assumption between rebuilds; do not add independent eligibility-refresh behavior.
        ✔ Cache normalized search text and immutable metadata; recompute the fresh inventory candidate union and allocations on opening/relevant changes, without an incremental inventory cache or per-render computation.
    ✔ Render only visible tiles for large catalogues and release GUI/render resources when the panel or world closes.
        ✔ Remove event subscriptions and dispose any feature patches/network lifecycle resources according to existing patterns.
        ✔ Discard stale deferred computations and requests during inventory/catalogue changes or closure; keep mutable game data on its owning thread.
        ✔ Verify repeated open/close and world transitions do not accumulate callbacks or render resources.
    ✔ Verify bounded rendering and change-driven recomputation with focused tests; do not invent unsupported compatibility/runtime-cost claims.

Completion criteria:

    ✔ Lifecycle tests/evidence cover rebuild, close/reopen, stale-result rejection, and owning-thread access.
    ✔ Offscreen tiles are not rendered and allocation does not run once per frame without relevant changes.
    ✔ Focused tests establish bounded visible rendering and no inventory allocation work on unchanged render frames.

### Phase 6 working evidence and traceability (2026-10-29)

Status: complete after implementation, second source review, delegated feature verification and a fresh independent read-only completion audit. The audit independently consulted governing documents, implementation/dependencies, native sources and the installed finalization sequence and found no required-now issues, partial requirements or material evidence gaps. The user explicitly removed mandatory catalogue benchmarks/measurement-derived thresholds and confirmed world-loading-only rebuilds; the proposal and this phase reflect those decisions. No registry observer, recipe-identity/version check or incremental inventory-result cache was added.

Controlling sources consulted: inherited [proposal](./CraftingMenuProposal.md), especially Scope, Player experience, Relevance and availability, Proposed implementation boundaries and Catalogue lifecycle and performance; Phase 1 native lifecycle evidence and Phases 2-5 state/allocation/native-operation contracts; [repository instructions](../.github/copilot-instructions.md) and [project.todo](./project.todo) conventions. Native API/survival source revisions remain 63d33f70/849fa8c; installed concrete GUI/event methods were inspected in 1.22.7.0.

- World catalogue/metadata tasks -> proposal Scope and Catalogue lifecycle; [IClientEventAPI.cs](../../../vsapi/Client/API/IClientEventAPI.cs), LevelFinalize/LeaveWorld -> CraftingMenuSystem builds and retains one catalogue/policy/allocator context at world finalization, clears it and open dialogs at world exit, and reuses only immutable metadata across popout openings. Installed ClientSystemStartup.HandleLevelFinalize calls native client systems, including SystemModHandler's AssetsFinalize phase, before TriggerLevelFinalize; it also performs GL work on that client/render path. Existing CraftingCatalogue entries retain normalized SearchText and immutable preview/ingredient metadata. A new CraftingMenuSession performs fresh capture/allocation for every opening; selected outputs, explicit alternatives and inventory results are not world caches. Lifecycle tests cover empty/ready cache state, reuse, fresh stock on reopening, selection reset, new-world recipe replacement and owning-thread initialization. Inventory candidate/allocator semantics are unchanged.
- Panel/world cleanup tasks -> proposal native UI/lifecycle and owning-thread boundaries; [GuiDialog.cs](../../../vsapi/Client/UI/Dialog/GuiDialog.cs), TryClose/OnClosed/Dispose; [GuiElementContainer.cs](../../../vsapi/Client/UI/Elements/Impl/Interactive/GuiElementContainer.cs), native child/resource disposal; existing AlloyCalculator lifecycle conventions -> dialog disposal removes the system's dictionary entry immediately through its Disposed event, in addition to releasing coordinator, parent/world/session subscriptions, composer/tooltip resources and LoadedGuis registration. System disposal detaches static composition and world-finalization/exit handlers. Repeated native-dialog tests cover parent close, world exit and direct disposal across five cycles per path, idempotence, removal from native GUI registration, and queued inventory callbacks being discarded. Existing coordinator tests retain coalescing, owner-thread checks, native inventory-close cleanup and refusal after disposal; no deferred recipe computation or custom network lifecycle is introduced.
- Bounded rendering/change-driven work tasks -> proposal visible-only rendering and native controls; [GuiElementTextButton.cs](../../../vsapi/Client/UI/Elements/Impl/Interactive/Controls/GuiElementTextButton.cs) and native container composition -> existing viewport-sized button recycling remains bounded independently of catalogue size. Item rendering now rejects zero-area clip-boundary contact as well as fully offscreen tiles. A real Cairo/native-control composition test uses 256 recipes with a six-button pool, observes exactly four icon-render calls in a 96x96 viewport and six for partial rows after scrolling 24 pixels, tracks all 28 native texture handles and releases them across five compose/render/dispose cycles. Multiple unchanged render frames and search/scroll operations leave inventory capture count at one. This is native control/Cairo composition with mocked GPU upload/render endpoints, not a live OpenGL or timing claim.

Observed delegated verification: dotnet test .\VanillaExpanded.Tests\VanillaExpanded.Tests.csproj --no-restore --filter "FullyQualifiedName~CraftingMenu" passes 172 tests (the original 165 plus seven new lifecycle/rendering cases), zero failures/skips, successful production/test compilation and no new CraftingMenu warnings. git diff --check and localization JSON parsing pass. Second review checked the source contract, world/cache ownership, disposal callback ownership, native notification queue cancellation, thread affinity, unchanged allocator/transfer paths and strict clip-boundary culling. Live layout, actual OpenGL presentation, multiplayer and packaging remain Phase 7; mandatory representative timing acceptance was removed by the user, not deferred.

### Menu-opening performance follow-up (2026-10-29)

The user reported an approximately 20-second live-client opening freeze after Phase 6. The earlier bounded-rendering tests did not measure that path. This follow-up reduces synchronous evaluation work rather than moving game-owned recipe/inventory access off-thread or introducing an incremental inventory cache.

- Retain the native ingredient-index snapshot with the world catalogue and evaluate predicates in native order. Do not narrow custom collectible semantics by class/id: a native exact predicate can delegate to an overridden `Satisfies` that accepts other IDs.
- Share full stack-identity predicate results only within one fresh inventory evaluation, including ordinary/bulk native winner checks. Identity retains quantity, persistent attributes and typed temporary attributes. Discard these results at the end of each evaluation; do not retain inventory-derived matches across refreshes.
- Skip combinatorial solving for recipes outside the attention union after validating support and constructing their complete one-execution deficits. Reuse cloned ingredient predicates within one solve. Preserve fresh action-time evaluation and native output/winner validation.
- Replace per-assignment solver LINQ/grouping/delegate/sort allocations with bounded scratch spans and loops. The first complete ordinary allocation in stable enumeration already wins its ordinary objectives; bulk optimization still considers all feasible variant assignments under the existing lexicographic objectives.
- Opening emits a debug timing line separating layout/composition from inventory evaluation and recording displayed output count, so remaining live-client stalls can be localized without claiming that a synthetic test reproduces the player's world.

Diagnostic evidence before the subsequent gate removal (Windows, .NET SDK 10.0.401/net10.0, installed 1.22.7 references, Debug tests): a 2,000-recipe, nine-position fixture with 100 attention candidates originally took 959.9 ms and allocated 93.42 MiB. The isolated fresh-testhost run took 93.8 ms and 18.53 MiB (about 10x faster, 80% fewer allocated bytes). A nine-position/eight-incompatible-variant wildcard case dropped from 266.5 ms/261.19 MiB to 39.7 ms/1.98 MiB. Timings are diagnostic observations, not numerical acceptance thresholds, modpack benchmarks or proof of the reported live world's latency.

Original performance validation: all 180 CraftingMenu tests passed. The original exact-index narrowing and cached reflection classification were then removed at the user's request; those are not current architecture or acceptance requirements.

After removing the gates and ID bucketing, a focused two-profile run measured 46.2 ms/18.51 MiB for the 2,000-recipe fixture and 42.4 ms/1.99 MiB for the repeated-wildcard fixture. These remain synthetic observations, not live-client latency guarantees.

User-approved reflection-gate removal: delete collectible method inspection/type caching and private recipe-event subscription inspection, including their localized refusal. Do not reject source/ingredient/output collectibles merely for overrides or behaviors. Remove the dependent exact-ID bucketing rather than replacing reflection with another compatibility whitelist. Preserve per-evaluation native-predicate sharing, solver improvements, native trait checks, native complete-layout/winner validation and actual transfer restrictions. Refreshed validation: all 180 CraftingMenu tests pass, zero failures/skips and no new CraftingMenu warnings. Updated regressions accept custom event subscriptions, collectible behaviors, custom durability callbacks without invoking consumption, and native movement callback overrides. Native predicate order, custom cross-ID satisfaction, per-refresh sharing/reset, quantity/typed temporary attributes, exhaustive allocation objectives, transfer conservation and native GUI lifecycle remain covered. Live-client opening time must be rechecked; prior independent audits predate these follow-ups.

## Phase 7: Validate end-to-end behavior and deliver the feature

Dependencies: Phases 1 through 6.
Outcome: source, automated, package, and user-run live evidence separately establish the proposal's acceptance contract.

    ☐ Run focused allocation, ranking, transfer, and interaction tests together using the repository's configured runner.
        ☐ Run the smallest build/test commands covering the feature; escalate broader regression checks when failures or shared-surface changes justify them.
        ☐ Distinguish isolated unit evidence from actual integration/E2E evidence; mocks alone do not prove multiplayer authority.
    ☐ Build/package through .\build.ps1 and the existing Cake workflow.
        ☐ Verify compilation, applicable JSON validation, and inclusion of required code/assets/localization in the release package.
        ☐ Do not add migrations or compatibility scaffolding unless the verified implementation requires them; document and verify any such necessary work before closure.
    ☐ Obtain user-run live-client evidence using the built package.
        ☐ Verify browse/search discoverability, output variants, alternative selection/release, unknown/unsupported entries, empty results, and dynamic-output behavior.
        ☐ Verify hotbar/carried/grid sourcing, excluded inventories, all missing quantities, and updates after inventory changes.
        ☐ Verify ordinary partial placement and maximum-stack shift placement for shaped/shapeless, repeated, wildcard, restricted, attribute-sensitive, tool, and non-stackable cases.
        ☐ Verify full-inventory/local preflight refusals leave state unchanged; locally observed native refusals retain accepted moves, stop unsent work, let in-flight operations resolve, and communicate interruption without inferring server outcomes.
        ☐ Verify manual output-taking/crafting still works after placement, including permitted surplus and tool durability behavior.
        ☐ Verify search focus, scrolling, hover/keyboard tooltips, stable hovered tiles, parent movement/closing, UI scaling, and small-screen placement.
        ☐ Verify actual multiplayer authoritative updates, competing inventory changes, stale/duplicate requests, and UI reconciliation without loss, duplication, drops, or unauthorized transfers.
        ☐ Recheck lifecycle and representative modded-catalogue behavior in the live client; report compatibility only for exercised cases.
    ☐ Update directly related user documentation with controls, grid-only scope, eligible sources, unsupported states, and safe-placement limitations.
        ☐ Remove feature-owned temporary fixtures/scaffolding from production wiring and clean up temporary test artifacts.
        ☐ Record remaining limitations honestly; any proposed deferral of a required behavior must receive explicit approval and an authoritative proposal/plan update.

Completion criteria:

    ☐ Required tests and package build succeed with identifiable commands/results.
    ☐ User-run live checks have recorded outcomes; unexecuted cases remain unchecked rather than inferred from unit tests.
    ☐ Every proposal acceptance criterion has an explicit passing evidence reference or an approved, documented scope change.

## Final acceptance

    ☐ Every eligible resolved grid output is discoverable, alternatives remain accessible, and selected-recipe data stays consistent.
    ☐ Availability/relevance/one-execution deficits use shared allocation, include existing grid quantities once, and update after relevant changes.
    ☐ Unsupported entries remain inspectable with unknown state and deterministic ordering, without invented scores/deficits or placement.
    ☐ Ordinary placement and joint lexicographic shift placement preserve native recipe, stack, attribute, tool, and layout semantics.
    ☐ Local preflight refusals leave original state intact. Native partial refusals/competing updates cannot lose, duplicate, automatically drop, or incorrectly consume items; accepted moves are retained and explicitly reconciled without unsafe replay.
    ☐ Existing manual crafting/output-taking and usable panel/search/tooltip/small-screen behavior are verified in the live client.
    ☐ Source verification, focused tests, actual integration/multiplayer evidence, package validation, bounded-rendering/lifecycle verification, documentation, and cleanup are complete.
    ☐ Every requirement is implemented or covered by an explicitly approved proposal/plan amendment; no unapproved deferral is hidden by a checked completion box.
